Chapter: MessageFraming
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256

 Length-prefixed deterministic serialization for trust network
 messages. Encodes integers as little-endian, text and byte arrays
 as length-prefixed, and frames each message with a type tag.

 We say:

Section: Wire Format

 Length-prefixed deterministic serialization for trust network messages.
 Format: [4 bytes: length][1 byte: type tag][N bytes: fields]
 All integers are little-endian. Text is length-prefixed (4 bytes len + bytes).
 Byte arrays (keys, hashes, signatures) are length-prefixed.

  frame-encode : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  frame-encode (type-tag) (body) =
    let total = 1 + list-length body
    in frame-le32 total & [type-tag] & body

  frame-decode-length : List Integer -> Integer
  frame-decode-length (bs) =
    list-at bs 0 + bit-shl (list-at bs 1) 8
    + bit-shl (list-at bs 2) 16 + bit-shl (list-at bs 3) 24

  frame-decode-tag : List Integer -> Integer
  frame-decode-tag (bs) = list-at bs 4

  frame-decode-body : List Integer -> List Integer
  frame-decode-body (bs) =
    let len = frame-decode-length bs
    in frame-slice bs 5 (4 + len)

Section: Serialization Primitives

  frame-le32 : Integer -> List Integer
  frame-le32 (v) = [bit-and v 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 24) 255]

  frame-le64 : Integer -> List Integer
  frame-le64 (v) = frame-le32 v & frame-le32 (bit-shru v 32)

  frame-encode-text : Text -> List Integer
  frame-encode-text (s) =
    let bytes = frame-text-bytes s 0 (text-length s) []
    in frame-le32 (list-length bytes) & bytes

  frame-text-bytes : Text, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  frame-text-bytes (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i == len then acc
    else frame-text-bytes s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (char-code (char-at s i)))

  frame-decode-text : List Integer, Integer -> FrameTextResult
  frame-decode-text (bs) (offset) =
    let len = frame-read-le32 bs offset
    in let text = frame-bytes-to-text bs (offset + 4) len ""
    in FrameTextResult { value = text, next-offset = __narrow (offset + 4 + len) }

  FrameTextResult = record {
    value : Text,
    next-offset : Integer between 0 and 4294967295
  }

  frame-bytes-to-text : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  frame-bytes-to-text (bs) (i) (remaining) (acc) =
    if remaining <= 0 then acc
    else frame-bytes-to-text bs (i + 1) (remaining - 1) (acc & char-to-text (code-to-char (list-at bs i)))

  frame-read-le32 : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  frame-read-le32 (bs) (off) =
    list-at bs off + bit-shl (list-at bs (off + 1)) 8
    + bit-shl (list-at bs (off + 2)) 16 + bit-shl (list-at bs (off + 3)) 24

  frame-read-le64 : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  frame-read-le64 (bs) (off) =
    frame-read-le32 bs off + bit-shl (frame-read-le32 bs (off + 4)) 32

  frame-encode-bytes : List Integer -> List Integer
  frame-encode-bytes (bs) = frame-le32 (list-length bs) & bs

  frame-decode-bytes : List Integer, Integer -> FrameBytesResult
  frame-decode-bytes (bs) (offset) =
    let len = frame-read-le32 bs offset
    in FrameBytesResult { value = frame-slice bs (offset + 4) (offset + 4 + len), next-offset = __narrow (offset + 4 + len) }

  FrameBytesResult = record {
    value : List Integer,
    next-offset : Integer between 0 and 4294967295
  }

Section: Message Type Tags

  tag-propose : Integer = 1

  tag-grant : Integer = 2

  tag-deny : Integer = 3

  tag-explain : Integer = 4

  tag-narrate : Integer = 5

  tag-interrupt : Integer = 6

  tag-handoff : Integer = 7

  tag-hello : Integer = 8

  tag-challenge : Integer = 9

  tag-prove : Integer = 10

  tag-accept : Integer = 11

  tag-annotate : Integer = 12

  tag-verdict : Integer = 13

  tag-reject : Integer = 14

  tag-sync-offer : Integer = 15

  tag-sync-reply : Integer = 16

 A sync offer and its reply are BULK reconciliation: the asker offers everything
 it holds and the answerer sends back everything the offer did not name. These
 two are the opposite, and that is why they exist rather than reusing those. A
 quotation names one hash, so the ask is one hash and the answer is one work or
 nothing. The offer verb would have the asker publish its whole store to ask a
 single question, and the answerer serve its whole store to anyone who asked --
 least authority in reverse.

  tag-work-request : Integer = 17

  tag-work-reply : Integer = 18

 Locate is the question one layer above work-request: not "give me this work"
 but "who holds it". The two verbs stay separate because their answers are
 different kinds of thing. A work is content and is checkable by its own hash;
 an address is a hint and is checkable by nothing. Folding them into one verb
 would mean a registry had to hold every work it can point at, which is the
 entire cost the indirection exists to avoid.

 So a locate reply is worth exactly what a rumour is worth, and is treated that
 way: the asker still fetches by hash from whoever it names, and still checks
 what comes back against the hash it asked for. A registry that lies costs a
 wasted round trip and cannot cause a wrong work to be accepted. That is the
 argument that lets the transport be untrusted, applied one level further out.

  tag-locate-request : Integer = 19

  tag-locate-reply : Integer = 20

 A peer tells a registry what it holds, so that a locate can be answered out of
 memory. This exists because the registry MUST NOT have to ask: answering a
 locate by probing peers means opening an outbound connection while an inbound
 one is still open, and a guest cannot do that -- the reply to the inbound
 connection is emitted and never delivered. Announcing inverts it. The peer
 dials at boot, when it has nothing inbound open, and the registry only ever
 accepts. The nesting is not avoided by careful sequencing; it becomes
 unreachable.

 An announcement is a rumour like a locate reply, and is worth the same: it says
 an address claims a digest. Nothing verifies the claim and nothing needs to,
 because the asker still fetches by hash and still checks what comes back. A
 peer that announces works it does not hold costs a wasted round trip.

 It goes stale by construction -- a peer can die or drop a work -- and that is
 affordable for the same reason. "Peer P holds digest D" is nearly monotonic,
 since a digest is immutable and the claim stays true until P goes away, and the
 fetch path already walks candidates in turn and tolerates a miss.

  tag-announce-request : Integer = 21

  tag-announce-reply : Integer = 22

Section: Content Hashing

  frame-content-hash : List Integer -> List Integer
  frame-content-hash (body) = sha256 body

Section: Helpers

  frame-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  frame-slice (xs) (start) (stop) = frame-slice-loop xs start stop []

  frame-slice-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  frame-slice-loop (xs) (i) (stop) (acc) =
    if i >= stop then acc
    else frame-slice-loop xs (i + 1) stop (list-push acc (list-at xs i))

